#uuid "fa030baa-01fb-586b-9925-167b6b320ec5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbdd6305-e297-593d-89f4-fb7ada8ade14" :question/math-academy-id "q-73455" :question/problem "![](images/de/de39663db59e42565c7d69d8a44a4cb95f14af0f4946b77cfc42eac02f7d2786.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\nI. $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[-\\pi ,0)$\n\nIII. $[1,\\pi ]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is closed,but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-\\pi ,0)$ is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1a7dfce-6bc6-58f9-beef-bc1c118e1ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468004} {:answer/id #uuid "41f032ab-2bf6-598d-a02f-0c37ae9c222c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :a