b/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b5c08f3-5fbe-50f0-8681-6cf3ebc354c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73463" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4de4aa4956b0b70e6e08c9f0188944469270ab19501194ce897e2af9411a0d98.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n- $[0,\\pi ]$\n- $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e532f7-a8d4-5ad0-87fa-e0abb07a53ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} {:answer/id #uuid "166b32ef-7d9c-5749-b3c9-7228a4faa2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text